Cell["\<\
{EdgeCount[Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2}], Edge[{1, 2}]}]],
  Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2}], Edge[{1, 2}]}] === Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2}]}]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"ffa60267-93dd-5e49-98b7-fc72533cc3c9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"2", ",", "False"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"1b058fd1-9049-8141-9444-8b1c1314fa50"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "Edges are sorted into a canonical order when the hypergraph is built, which \
is what makes === on hypergraphs agree with equality of multisets. The same \
is done for the vertex list. So a Hypergraph expression is always in a normal \
form, and any two ways of writing the same hypergraph produce literally the \
same expression."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"5bc27734-c5da-874d-b3ba-a06fe066df47"],

Cell["4.  How a hypergraph is drawn", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"7b526f2a-38d3-9547-988d-af080dfd77fb"],

Cell["\<\
The drawing is the reference paclet's: the same spring layout of an auxiliary \
graph, the same blue palette, binary edges as arcs, and edges of arity three \
and above as filled concave polygons through their vertices. The plot is also \
available on its own as HypergraphPlot.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2a1d28e1-c7df-ec44-9d01-728929e8a3c7"],

Cell["\<\
HypergraphPlot[Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2, 3}, \"Unordered\"], Edge[{3, 4}],
                             Edge[{1, 4, 5}, \"Cyclic\"]}], ImageSize -> 300]\
\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"ea629003-2949-044f-8d4d-ddefcef8c1e0"],

